<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-1.jpg" alt="https://www.google.com/books/edition/Modern_Puzzles_and_how_to_Solve_Them/FS8PAAAAQAAJ?hl=en&amp;gbpv=1&amp;pg=PA54&amp;printsec=frontcover" width="898" height="671" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-74692" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-1.jpg 898w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-1-600x448.jpg 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-1-300x224.jpg 300w" sizes="(max-width: 898px) 100vw, 898px" /></p>
<p>By Henry Dudeney:</p>
<blockquote><p>
Here is a queer new puzzle that I know will interest my readers considerably. The four draughtsmen are shown exactly as they stood on a square chequered board &#8212; not necessarily eight squares by eight &#8212; but the ink with which the board was drawn was evanescent, so that all the diagram except the men has disappeared. How many squares were there in the board and how am I to reconstruct it? I know that each man stood in the middle of a square, one on the edge of each side of the board and no man in a corner. It is a real puzzle, until you hit on the method of solution, and then to get the correct answer is absurdly easy.
</p></blockquote>

