</p>
<blockquote><p>
Draw a line from A to D. Then draw CE perpendicular to AD, and equal in length to AD. Then E will be the centre of another square. Draw a line from E to B and extend it on both sides. Also draw a line FG through C and parallel to EB and the lines through A and D perpendicular to EB and FG. Now, as H is the centre of a corner square, we can mark off the length HE all round the square and we find the board is 10 &#215; 10.</p>
<p><img decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-2.jpg" alt="https://www.google.com/books/edition/Modern_Puzzles_and_how_to_Solve_Them/FS8PAAAAQAAJ?hl=en&amp;gbpv=1&amp;pg=RA1-PA144&amp;printsec=frontcover" width="810" height="727" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-74693" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-2.jpg 810w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-2-600x539.jpg 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-17-the-four-draughtsmen-2-300x269.jpg 300w" sizes="(max-width: 810px) 100vw, 810px" /></p>
<p>If the size of the men were not given we might subdivide into more squares, but the men would be too large for the squares. As the distance between the centres of squares is the same as the width of the squares, we can now complete the board with ease, as shown in the diagram inset.
</p></blockquote>
<p>[The inset diagram is hard to read, but if rows are numbered and files lettered, I believe the men are on e1, a5, j7, and d10.]</p>
